In article <1992Nov29.085951.12854@wisipc.weizmann.ac.il> fedimit@wisipc.weizmann.ac.il (Dan Nabutovsky) writes:
>In article <ByB1HF.G5L@cs.columbia.edu> vh@cs.columbia.edu (Vasileios Hatzivassiloglou) writes:
>>I always try to genocide most of the monsters in the following list :
>
>>1. gelatinous cubes (can eat valuable organic objects; can paralyze you and
>>                     allow another monster to hack you to death).
>But their corpses are healthy!
>

I'm not sure what you mean by that. If you mean that they can confer poison
resistance, I think that by the time you start meeting them you should already
have got it from some other monster. If you mean that they can be eaten, many
other monsters can provide food without being so dangerous. Gelatinous cubes
are very hard for a mid-level character to deal with because of their ability
to paralyze, and they can eat really useful stuff, so I can't see any 
justification for leaving them alive.

>>8. cockatrices (you can be protected, but they always offer an opportunity
>>                for a stupid death).
>Don't genocide them. Their are helpful for killing Wizard of Yendor.
>Almost a need.
>

I never found that trick necessary. By the time you meet the Wiz, you should
be protected enough and with a good enough weapon so that he shouldn't be
much of a problem. You can't really depend on a cockatrice corpse as your
weapon, as it might rot away when you need it most.

>>9. rock moles (they eat metal).
>So what?
>

As I said before, they eat metal. So you can lose that nice piece of armor
that is generated on the floor of a maze, or if you are lucky enough to find
a temple, the ability to boost your AC with gold offerings.

>10. Floating eyes.

Remember that floating eyes are the only monsters that confer the telepathy
intrinsic, which you might lose if you attack a peaceful monster. So I prefer
to keep them alive; they are not much of a problem if you are careful, or if
you get an amulet/shield of reflection.

Vasilis Hatzivassiloglou.



